var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Physics","page":"Physics","text":"As shown in examples/1degree.jl, the default configuration is an ocean–sea ice model forced with the JRA55 atmosphere and land, configured with (semi-)validated closures. The model is initialised with WOA climatological temperature and salinity, and ECCO sea ice concentration and thickness.\n\nusing NumericalOcean\nusing NumericalEarth\nusing Oceananigans\nusing Oceananigans.Units\nusing Dates\nusing CUDA\n\nauto_config_directories!()\n\ngrid = ORCA1(GPU())\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid)\n\npickup_restart!(simulation, DateTime(1958, 1, 1))\n\nomip_diagnostics!(simulation)\ncheckpointer!(simulation)\n\nrun!(simulation)\n\nThe grid can be any global grid, but automatic setup is only checked for ORCA1, ORCA025, and ORCA12, which are nominally 1degree, 14degree, and 112degree.\n\nThe whole model is set up through forced_ocean_simulation, so everything can be modified by passing arguments. For example, to add a passive tracer c and release it in a circle of radius 1° around (0°E, 0°N) for the first day (t in seconds):\n\nc_release = Forcing((λ, φ, z, t) -> (sqrt(λ^2 + φ^2) < 1) & (t < 86400))\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid;\n                                     tracers = (:c, ),\n                                     forcing = (; c = c_release))\n\nSee the forced_ocean_simulation docstring for all of the options.","title":"Physics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public","page":"API","text":"","title":"Public"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Internals","page":"API","text":"","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.GaussianInjection","page":"API","text":"A release into the surface cells with a Gaussian footprint, as a forcing (in tracer units per second) on the tracer it is passed for, e.g. forcing = (; Alk2 = GaussianInjection(grid, (-49.5, 0), 5 * 365days)).\n\ntotal_injection (tracer units × m³, e.g. 1000e12 mmol = 1 Tmol of alkalinity) is spread over the surface cells within cutoff (m) of center = (λ, φ) (degrees), weighted by exp(-r² / 2σ²) with σ in m, at a constant rate from start_time to start_time + duration (s of model time).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.GaussianInjection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.GaussianInjectionLowMemory","page":"API","text":"GaussianInjection with the footprint recomputed every evaluation rather than stored in a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.GaussianInjectionLowMemory"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.MaunaLoaCO₂","page":"API","text":"MaunaLoaCO₂(architecture = CPU();\n            dataset = MaunaLoa(),\n            name = :carbon_dioxide,\n            start_date = DateTime(1958, 1, 1),\n            end_date = DateTime(2018, 1, 1),\n            dates = filter(date -> start_date ≤ date < end_date, all_dates(dataset, name)),\n            dir = nothing,\n            FT = Float64,\n            period = forcing_period(MultiYearJRA55(), start_date, end_date))\n\nDownload the Scripps MaunaLoa record and return atmospheric CO₂ dry-air mole fraction (ppm) as a FieldTimeSeries located at (Nothing, Nothing, Nothing) — a single value per time, uniform in space — on a Flat, Flat, Flat grid. Sample it like any other FieldTimeSeries:\n\nusing Dates, Oceananigans\n\nxCO₂ = MaunaLoaCO₂(arch; start_date = DateTime(1958, 3, 15))\n\nxCO₂[1, 1, 1, Time(clock.time)] # ppm at the model's current time\n\nstart_date is the date model time zero represents — the simulation's own start date, not the record's, whenever the two differ.\n\nThe samples are placed at evenly spaced mid-month times (times is a range), starting half a month after the first day of the first selected month, rather than on the record's 15th-of-the-month dates. This keeps times a range on the GPU, so interpolating the series does not copy it to the host each time step; each sample is within ~2 days of its month's actual midpoint.\n\nTime indexing is Cyclical. By default it takes the months from start_date to end_date and cycles them with the multi-year JRA55 forcing forced_ocean_simulation builds for the same dates (21915 days for 1958–2018, see forcing_period), so the CO₂ wraps back to 1958 with the atmosphere. The samples are spread evenly over the period, period / Nt apart (30.4375 days for 1958–2018), with the same spacing across the seam. Pass e.g. period = 365days with one year of dates to cycle in step with repeat-year forcing, or period = nothing for the mean Gregorian month (365.2425 / 12 days) spacing and a period of that times the number of samples.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\ndataset: the MaunaLoa dataset, carrying the URL the CSV is fetched from.\nname: which series to read — :carbon_dioxide (Mauna Loa, the default),         :south_pole_carbon_dioxide, or :mean_carbon_dioxide.\nstart_date: the date corresponding to model time zero. Default: 1958-01-01, as forced_ocean_simulation.\nend_date: the end of the forcing cycle. Default: 2018-01-01, as forced_ocean_simulation.\ndates: the window of the record to use. Default: the months from start_date up to end_date.\ndir: where the CSV is cached. Default: the NumericalEarth download cache.\nFT: element type of the returned series. Default: Float64.\nperiod: cycle length in seconds, or nothing for the span of dates plus one final mean month.           Default: the multi-year JRA55 forcing's period from start_date to end_date.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.MaunaLoaCO₂"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.add_output_writer!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"add_output_writer!(simulation, name, writer; async)\n\n\nAdd writer to simulation.output_writers as name. With async = true it is wrapped in an AsyncJLD2Writer, which compresses and writes on a background task (so needs julia --threads ≥ 2), and a callback is added to write anything still queued at the end of run!. Call flush_output!(simulation) in a finally around run! too, so that an error does not lose queued writes. A Checkpointer waits for the queued writes before it saves, so add it after the writers (see checkpointer!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.add_output_writer!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.checkpointer!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"checkpointer!(\n    simulation;\n    checkpoint_interval,\n    output_dir,\n    filename_prefix\n)\n\n\nAdd a checkpointer of the coupled model, for run!(simulation; pickup = true). Call it after the diagnostics: writers run in the order they were added, so at an iteration where an output and a checkpoint coincide the output is written first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.checkpointer!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.forced_ocean_simulation-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"forced_ocean_simulation(\n    grid;\n    arch,\n    backend_size,\n    start_date,\n    end_date,\n    atmosphere_tracers,\n    staging,\n    jra55_dataset,\n    jra55_dir,\n    forcing_cycle,\n    climatology_cycle,\n    land,\n    eddy_closure,\n    background_diffusivity,\n    vertical_closure,\n    horizontal_viscosity,\n    closures,\n    salt_restoring,\n    chlorophyll,\n    radiative_forcing,\n    momentum_advection,\n    biogeochemistry,\n    tracers,\n    forcing,\n    boundary_conditions,\n    coriolis,\n    river_tracers,\n    river_fluxes,\n    dust_deposition,\n    time_discretization,\n    tracer_advection,\n    ocean,\n    sea_ice_immersed_latitudes,\n    sea_ice_grid,\n    sea_ice,\n    atmosphere,\n    sea_ice_albedo,\n    radiation,\n    atmosphere_correction,\n    radiation_correction,\n    biogeochemistry_interface_kwargs,\n    air_kinematic_viscosity,\n    similarity_form,\n    atmosphere_ocean_fluxes,\n    atmosphere_sea_ice_fluxes,\n    sea_ice_ocean_heat_flux,\n    ice_meltwater_enthalpy,\n    saturation_enhancement,\n    humidity,\n    interfaces,\n    Δt,\n    stop_time,\n    progress_frequency,\n    T_and_S_initialisation_dataset,\n    initialise_bgc\n) -> Oceananigans.Simulations.Simulation{ML, _A, _B, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractDiagnostic}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractOutputWriter}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.Simulations.Callback}, Float64, Bool, Nothing} where {ML<:(NumericalEarth.EarthSystemModels.EarthSystemModel{R, _A, L, I, O} where {R<:NumericalEarth.Radiations.PrescribedRadiation, _A, L<:NumericalEarth.Lands.PrescribedLand, I<:(Oceananigans.Simulations.Simulation{ML, Float64, ST, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractDiagnostic}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractOutputWriter}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.Simulations.Callback}, Float64, Bool, Nothing} where {ML, ST}), O<:(Oceananigans.Simulations.Simulation{_A, _B, _C, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractDiagnostic}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.AbstractOutputWriter}, OrderedCollections.OrderedDict{Symbol, Oceananigans.Simulations.Callback}, Float64, Bool, Nothing} where {_A, _B, _C})}), _A, _B}\n\n\nBuild a JRA55-forced ocean–sea-ice Simulation on grid, optionally carrying biogeochemistry with OAEMIP's initial conditions, rivers and iron dust. Extra passive tracers are added to (:T, :S). boundary_conditions are passed to ocean_simulation, which merges them side by side with its defaults; an immersed condition for u or v replaces the default one whole, so it must carry the bottom drag too. A coriolis replaces ocean_simulation's default (nothing keeps it), e.g. HydrostaticSphericalCoriolis(scheme = ConsistentAreaEnstrophyConserving()) with ORCA1(arch; immersed_bottom = PartialCellBottom).\n\nModel time zero is start_date. The JRA55 forcing cycles every forcing_cycle seconds (see forcing_period: 21915 days for 1958–2018, 365 for repeat-year forcing) and the climatologies (WOA salinity, SeaWiFS chlorophyll, iron dust) every climatology_cycle (see climatology_period: 365.25 days, or 365), so all stay in phase with each other and the calendar from cycle to cycle, the climatologies to within 0.75 days. Cycle any atmosphere_tracers the same way, e.g. MaunaLoaCO₂(arch; dates, start_date, period = forcing_cycle).\n\nEvery progress_frequency iterations (nothing for never) the time, extrema of the state and wall time per step are logged, followed by a PROBE line of memory and GC use (unless ENV[\"OMIP_PROBE\"] = \"0\"). Its reductions synchronise the GPU, so keep it infrequent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.forced_ocean_simulation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.oaemip_diagnostics!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"oaemip_diagnostics!(\n    simulation;\n    surface_averaging_interval,\n    field_averaging_interval,\n    averaging_stride,\n    output_dir,\n    filename_prefix,\n    file_splitting_interval,\n    async\n)\n\n\nAdd OAEMIP's biogeochemical output writers (carbonate system, O₂ saturation, productivity, POP export and global carbon integrals) for a NutrientsPlanktonDetritus model with ImplicitProductivity, written in the background with async = true (see add_output_writer!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.oaemip_diagnostics!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.omip_diagnostics!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"omip_diagnostics!(\n    simulation;\n    field_mean_interval,\n    surface_averaging_interval,\n    field_averaging_interval,\n    averaging_stride,\n    output_dir,\n    filename_prefix,\n    file_splitting_interval,\n    async\n)\n\n\nAdd OMIPSimulations' surface, 3-D field and global-average output writers to simulation, written in the background with async = true (see add_output_writer!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.omip_diagnostics!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.pickup_restart!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"pickup_restart!(\n    simulation,\n    date;\n    start_date,\n    repeat_year,\n    dir,\n    prefix,\n    restart_start_date,\n    restart_end_date,\n    cycle_length,\n    cycle\n) -> Any\n\n\nSet simulation's model to the checkpoint of the OMIP spin-up closest to date, and its clock to that checkpoint's date as seconds since start_date (the start_date the simulation was built with).\n\nWith repeat_year = true (for RepeatYearJRA55 runs, with start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1)), the clock is instead set to the checkpoint's time of year since start_date, so e.g. pickup_restart!(simulation, DateTime(1990, 1, 1); start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1), repeat_year = true) starts from the spin-up's state closest to 1 Jan 1990 at the forcing's matching time of year.\n\nThe checkpoints are dir/prefix_iteration*.jld2, written by a run forced by MultiYearJRA55 from restart_start_date to restart_end_date, which it cycled every cycle_length seconds. The default, 21915 days + 3 hours for 1958–2018, is the period that run's forcing inferred for itself (see forcing_cycle_length); pass cycle_length = forcing_period(MultiYearJRA55(), restart_start_date, restart_end_date) (21915 days) for checkpoints from runs whose forcing is phase-locked by forced_ocean_simulation. A checkpoint's date is that of the atmospheric forcing it was last forced with. Only checkpoints from cycle (0-based, default: the last cycle present) are considered.\n\nThe new run's forcing is all phase-locked to the calendar, but in a checkpointed run with inferred periods only the 3-hourly JRA55 atmosphere and radiation sit at the dates assigned here: the rest drifted against them, so a state from its nth cycle was spun up under rivers n × 0.875 days behind where the new run puts them on the same date, WOA salinity restoring n × 15.125 days ahead and SeaWiFS chlorophyll n × 44.875 days behind, the climatologies a further day ahead for each 29 February (WOA) or behind for each 365-day year (SeaWiFS) since restart_start_date.\n\nOnly the model state is restored, and the iteration is set to zero, so the simulation initialises as a fresh run (output writer and callback schedules start from the new time, and the first step is forward Euler). Tracers and other state missing from the checkpoint (e.g. biogeochemical tracers when restarting from a physics-only spin-up) keep the values they were given when the simulation was built. simulation.stop_time is not changed: it is model time, so with repeat_year = false it must exceed the restored clock time.\n\nReturn the path of the checkpoint used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.pickup_restart!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.seawifs_chlorophyll_climatology-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"seawifs_chlorophyll_climatology(\n    grid;\n    years,\n    climatology_cycle,\n    dir,\n    load_cache\n) -> Oceananigans.OutputReaders.FieldTimeSeries{Oceananigans.Grids.Center, Oceananigans.Grids.Center, Nothing, Oceananigans.OutputReaders.Cyclical{Float64}, Oceananigans.OutputReaders.InMemory{Nothing}, I, D, G, ET, B, χ, Nothing, Nothing, @NamedTuple{}} where {I, D, G, ET, B, χ}\n\n\nSeaWiFS surface chlorophyll (mg m⁻³) on grid as a monthly climatology: each month the mean over years of that month's composite, each regridded and inpainted as Field(Metadatum(...), grid) does. SeaWiFSMonthly holds monthly composites of single months (1997-09 to 2010-12), not a climatology, so a single year carries that year's anomalies and gaps; the default years are the whole years of the record. The climatology sits on the times of the year 2000, as forced_ocean_simulation used the single year 2000 before, so that restarts (restart.jl) keep its phase, and cycles every climatology_cycle (the default is forced_ocean_simulation's for 1958–2018 JRA55).\n\nThe 12 monthly means are cached in dir as a plain Nx × Ny × 12 array and read back unless load_cache = false; computing them reads 12 × length(years) monthly files, which must already be in dir (or be downloadable). The cache is keyed by the grid's size only, so delete it after changing the land mask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.seawifs_chlorophyll_climatology"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.InterpolatedIronDust","page":"API","text":"InterpolatedIronDust(series; kwargs...)\n\nOceanBioME's IronDustDeposition of the surface dust series (a 2D FieldTimeSeries on the model grid) as a forcing for Fe, with the time interpolation of series done once per update into a 2D field rather than in every cell of the Fe tendency kernel. kwargs are passed to IronDustDeposition.\n\nEvaluated inline (IronDustDepositionForcing(series)), the time interpolation (a mod of the time, a search of times and two reads, in every thread) pushes the already heavy tracer tendency kernel over its register budget, slowing it ~10×. Here the kernel reads the surface dust from a 2D field; the vertical dissolution profile (znode and two exps) is still computed inline.\n\nThe surface dust is recomputed whenever the model's FieldTimeSeries are updated (every update_state!), so the forcing is the same as IronDustDepositionForcing(series; kwargs...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.InterpolatedIronDust"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.MaunaLoa","page":"API","text":"MaunaLoa(; url = MAUNA_LOA_URL)\n\nThe Scripps monthly mean atmospheric CO₂ record from Mauna Loa (:carbon_dioxide), with the South Pole (:south_pole_carbon_dioxide) and two-station mean (:mean_carbon_dioxide) series carried in the same file.\n\nValues are dry-air mole fractions in ppm (µmol/mol) on the Scripps '12' calibration scale, seasonally detrended, and stamped at midnight on the 15th of each month. They are not partial pressures: no sea-level-pressure or water-vapour correction is applied.\n\nThe record runs from March 1958 to March 2024. Months the stations did not observe are filled from a seasonally detrended spline fit; the file's Flag column records which, and is ignored here.\n\nurl must be a raw URL serving the CSV itself — for pastebin, https://pastebin.com/raw/<id> rather than the page that wraps it.\n\nSee MaunaLoaCO₂ for the time series a simulation consumes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.MaunaLoa"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.BGCInitDustIronDeposition","page":"API","text":"BGCInitDustIronDeposition(arch_or_grid = CPU(); kw...)\n\nSeasonal total dust deposition from Kok et al. (2021) via the BGC init climatology, as a FieldTimeSeries in kg dust m⁻² s⁻¹ (positive into the ocean) with Cyclical indexing, for IronDustDepositionForcing, which converts it to iron (3.5 wt% by default) dissolving with depth.\n\nPass the model grid to interpolate onto it (see BGCInitSolubleIronDeposition).\n\nNote: this is total dust (so soluble + insoluble iron). If the model also applies BGCInitSolubleIronDeposition as a separate surface flux, the soluble portion is double-counted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.BGCInitDustIronDeposition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.BGCInitSolubleIronDeposition","page":"API","text":"BGCInitSolubleIronDeposition(arch_or_grid = CPU(); kw...)\n\nMonthly soluble iron deposition from Hamilton et al. (2022) via the BGC init climatology, as a FieldTimeSeries in mmol Fe m⁻² s⁻¹ (negative into the ocean, i.e. a top boundary flux) with Cyclical indexing.\n\nPass the model grid (rather than an architecture, which gives the dataset's native 1ᵒ grid) to interpolate onto it, as is needed to use it as a boundary condition: the series is indexed with the model's i, j. All the snapshots are kept in memory by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.BGCInitSolubleIronDeposition"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.JRA55DataStagingCallback-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"JRA55DataStagingCallback(; source_dir, staging_dir, start_date, cycle_length = Inf, async = true)\n\nReturn a simulation callback that dynamically stages JRA55 yearly files from source_dir (slow disk) to staging_dir (fast scratch).\n\nAt each invocation the callback:\n\nReaps any background staging tasks that have finished\nIf the current year's data isn't ready yet, blocks until it is (safety fallback — should be rare if the callback fires often enough)\nSpawns background copies for the current and next year's files (if they aren't already on scratch or in flight)\nRemoves files from two or more years ago to free space\n\nEach year of JRA55 data is ~15–25 GB, so scratch holds at most ~50 GB at any time.\n\nThe year is that of the forcing at the model time, which cycles back to start_date every cycle_length seconds.\n\nAsync vs sync\n\nWith async = true (default), the callback returns immediately after Threads.@spawn, and the background task does the copy by launching an external cp process (run(cp …)), not Base.cp. This matters: the byte copy in Base.cp goes through a blocking sendfile ccall that is not gc-safe, so running it on a spawned thread parks that thread outside any safepoint and stalls the next stop-the-world GC — freezing the main loop for the entire copy. Shelling out keeps the byte-shuffling in a separate process and lets the Julia task wait on it cooperatively, so GC is never blocked. With Δt = 30 min, ~58 min of compute fits between year transitions, so a 20-min copy overlaps fully with compute.\n\nThe atomic mv(tmp, dst) inside atomic_replace! is the only point where concurrent readers can observe the file change. They see either the old symlink (slow read) or the new real copy (fast read), never a partial file. On MPI runs, the copy runs only on rank 0 in a background thread; cross-rank consistency comes from filesystem atomicity rather than an explicit barrier, so no MPI calls happen on the background thread.\n\nSet async = false to fall back to the original blocking behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.JRA55DataStagingCallback"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.RivR2OForcing-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"RivR2OForcing(grid; forced_tracers = (:DIC, :Alk, :PO₄, :DOP), kwargs...)\n\nThe RivR2O river loads as a 3D Forcing per tracer, nonzero only in the surface layer but evaluated at every level. Prefer RivR2OSurfaceFlux, which applies the same input as a top boundary flux; this is kept for comparison. Carbonate replicates (DIC1, Alk2, ...) in forced_tracers get the DIC/Alk input. See river_load_data for the keyword arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.RivR2OForcing"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.RivR2OSurfaceFlux-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"RivR2OSurfaceFlux(grid; forced_tracers = (:DIC, :Alk, :PO₄, :DOP), kwargs...)\n\nThe RivR2O river loads as a top FluxBoundaryCondition per tracer, -load / area at the river mouth cells (negative is into the ocean), for the additional_surface_fluxes of the ocean. With climatology = true the flux is constant and is computed once into a 2D Field; otherwise the loads are time interpolated at the surface each evaluation. Identical in the top cell to RivR2OForcing. Carbonate replicates (DIC1, Alk2, ...) in forced_tracers get the DIC/Alk input. See river_load_data for the keyword arguments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.RivR2OSurfaceFlux"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.checkpoint_times-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"checkpoint_times(dir, prefix) -> Tuple{Vector, Vector}\n\n\nReturn the (path, time) of every prefix_iteration*.jld2 checkpoint in dir, sorted by time (model seconds, read from the checkpointed clock).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.checkpoint_times"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.climatology_period-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"climatology_period(forcing_period) -> Any\n\n\nThe period (seconds) to cycle an annual climatology with in a run whose forcing cycles every forcing_period seconds: the mean length of the years in the cycle (365.25 days for 1958–2018, 365 days for repeat-year forcing), so it repeats a whole number of times per forcing cycle and has the same phase in every cycle.\n\nA 365-day climatology cannot follow leap days under Cyclical; at 365.25 days it stays within 0.75 days of the calendar. By itself it would cycle every 365 days (366 for a leap-year one, e.g. SeaWiFS 2000), running 15 days ahead of the calendar (45 days behind) by the end of 1958–2018 and starting each later cycle further out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.climatology_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.forcing_cycle_length-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"forcing_cycle_length(\n    dataset,\n    start_date,\n    end_date;\n    name\n) -> Any\n\n\nThe period (seconds) over which the JRA55 dataset forcing between start_date and end_date cycles, as its FieldTimeSeries infers it: the last node is included, so 1958–2018 cycles every 21915 days + 3 hours, not 21915 days. This is for the 3-hourly atmospheric state; the daily river fluxes cycle every 21916 days.\n\nThis is the cycle of runs built before forced_ocean_simulation set the period explicitly; later runs cycle every forcing_period (21915 days for 1958–2018).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.forcing_cycle_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.forcing_date-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"forcing_date(t, start_date, period) -> Any\n\n\nThe forcing date at model time t (seconds since start_date) in a run cycling its forcing every period seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.forcing_date"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.forcing_period-Tuple{NumericalEarth.DataWrangling.JRA55.MultiYearJRA55, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"forcing_period(\n    _::NumericalEarth.DataWrangling.JRA55.MultiYearJRA55,\n    start_date,\n    end_date\n) -> Any\n\n\nThe period (seconds) over which forced_ocean_simulation cycles the JRA55 dataset: the calendar span from start_date to end_date for MultiYearJRA55 (21915 days for 1958–2018), one 365-day year for RepeatYearJRA55.\n\nLeft to infer it, a multi-year series cycles over its nodes plus one interval, and its nodes run to the first on or after end_date: from 1958 to 2018 that is 21915 days + 3 hours for the 3-hourly variables and 21916 days for the daily rivers and icebergs, so the rivers slip 0.875 days a cycle against the atmosphere and both slip against the calendar. With this period the node past end_date is only ever the far end of the last interval before the wrap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.forcing_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.mauna_loa_co2-Tuple{Dates.AbstractDateTime}","page":"API","text":"mauna_loa_co2(date; dataset = MaunaLoa(), name = :carbon_dioxide)\n\nReturn the scalar CO₂ value (ppm) at date as a plain Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.mauna_loa_co2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.read_mauna_loa-Tuple{NumericalEarth.DataWrangling.Metadata{<:NumericalOcean.MaunaLoa}}","page":"API","text":"read_mauna_loa(metadata::MaunaLoaMetadata)\n\nReturn the (dates, values) of the series metadata names, read from the downloaded CSV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.read_mauna_loa"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.restart_clock_time-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"restart_clock_time(\n    checkpoint_date,\n    start_date;\n    repeat_year\n) -> Any\n\n\nThe model time (seconds since start_date) to give a checkpoint of the state on checkpoint_date. With repeat_year, checkpoint_date is first moved to start_date's year (by calendar date, so the 29th of February becomes the 28th), and the time wrapped into [0, 365days).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.restart_clock_time"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.select_restart-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"select_restart(\n    paths,\n    times,\n    date;\n    start_date,\n    repeat_year,\n    restart_start_date,\n    cycle_length,\n    cycle\n)\n\n\nChoose the checkpoint (from paths at model times) whose date in the forcing cycle is closest to date. The checkpointed run started on restart_start_date and cycled the forcing every cycle_length seconds; only checkpoints from cycle (0-based, default: the last one with any checkpoints) whose clock time for a run starting on start_date would be non-negative are considered.\n\nReturn (path, checkpoint_date, cycle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.select_restart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.setup_staging_directory-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"setup_staging_directory(source_dir, staging_dir)\n\nPopulate staging_dir with symlinks to every .nc file in source_dir. Reads go through symlinks (slow) until files are staged with stage_jra55_year!. Also:\n\nsweeps any leftover *.tmp from a killed prior run (half-written copies that would otherwise be mistaken for real copies);\nreplaces any real .nc file whose size doesn't match the source with a fresh symlink. A size mismatch means a prior run crashed mid-cp and left a truncated file — before this heal, HDF5 reads would fail with truncated file: eof = X, stored_eof = Y, and stage_jra55_year! would skip it (it only replaces symlinks).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.setup_staging_directory"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.stage_jra55!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"stage_jra55!(dataset)\n\nSymlink the JRA55 files from forcing_dir[] into staging_dir[], copy them for real when dataset is repeat-year forcing, and return staging_dir[] to read the forcing from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.stage_jra55!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.stage_jra55_year!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"stage_jra55_year!(source_dir, staging_dir, year)\n\nReplace symlinks in staging_dir with real copies for all JRA55 files matching year. Skips files that are already real copies. The replacement is atomic — a partial copy is never visible at dst, so concurrent PrefetchingBackend readers on background threads cannot race with the cp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.stage_jra55_year!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.stage_repeat_year_files!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"stage_repeat_year_files!(source_dir, staging_dir)\n\nReplace the symlinks to the repeat-year (RYF.*.nc) JRA55 files in staging_dir with real copies. Repeat-year forcing is one year of data reused every cycle, so it is copied once, at setup, before anything reads it; there is nothing to rotate, so no staging callback is needed. Files that are already real copies are skipped, and each copy is swapped in atomically (atomic_replace!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.stage_repeat_year_files!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#NumericalOcean.unstage_jra55_year!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"unstage_jra55_year!(source_dir, staging_dir, year)\n\nRemove real copies for year from staging_dir and restore symlinks to source_dir. Uses the same atomic swap as staging so a concurrent reader never sees a missing file at the swap point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NumericalOcean.unstage_jra55_year!"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Alkalinity-release-at-the-mouth-of-the-Amazon","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"This example releases 1 Tmol of alkalinity over one year at the mouth of the Amazon following the OAEMIP protocol, in the 1degree configuration with MITgcm-style DIC biogeochemistry and repeat-year (1990) JRA55 forcing. The release starts after 5 years and the run is 10 years long.\n\nIt needs a GPU and the forcing, restart and BGC data (see Forcing data), so it is not run when the documentation is built; the figures below are from a run on Bouchet.","title":"Alkalinity release at the mouth of the Amazon"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Packages","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"using NumericalOcean\nusing NumericalEarth\nusing Oceananigans\nusing Oceananigans.Units\nusing OceanBioME\nusing Dates\nusing CUDA","title":"Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Grid","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"The nominally 1degree eORCA1 tripolar grid on the GPU.\n\narch = GPU()\ngrid = ORCA1(arch)","title":"Grid"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Biogeochemistry","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"Light comes from the shortwave radiation in the atmosphere component, attenuated through the chlorophyll climatology (this model does not prognose phytoplankton).\n\nsurface_PAR = PARFromShortwave(grid)\nlight_attenuation = PrescribedAttenuationPAR(grid, surface_PAR;\n                                             attenuation = PrescribedChlorophyllAttenuation(first_color_fraction = 0.0),\n                                             attenuation_discrete_form = true)\n\nProductivity is implicit, as in the MITgcm DIC package. CarbonateSystem(2) carries two independent copies of the carbonate tracers (DIC1, Alk1, DIC2, Alk2); we release alkalinity into Alk2 only, so Alk2 - Alk1 and DIC2 - DIC1 are the perturbation.\n\nplankton = ImplicitProductivity(; maximum_community_productivity = 9.0 / 360days, # mmol P / m³ / s\n                                  light_half_saturation = 30.0, # W / m²\n                                  carbon_ratio = 117.0)         # mol C / mol P\n\nbiogeochemistry = MITgcmDIC(grid;\n                            plankton,\n                            light_attenuation,\n                            inorganic_carbon = CarbonateSystem(2),\n                            open_bottom = false,\n                            implicit_sinking = true,\n                            store_flux = true)\n\nAtmospheric pCO₂ is the 1990 Mauna Loa monthly record, repeated every 365 days to match the forcing.\n\npCO₂ = MaunaLoaCO₂(arch; dates = DateTime(1990, 1, 15):Month(1):DateTime(1990, 12, 15),\n                         start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1),\n                         period = 365days)","title":"Biogeochemistry"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Alkalinity-release","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"1 Tmol (1000e12 mmol) of alkalinity released over a year at the mouth of the Amazon (49.5°W, 0°N), starting after 5 years. The footprint is Gaussian with a 250 km standard deviation, cut off at 3 standard deviations, in the surface cells only.\n\nalkalinity_release = GaussianInjection(grid, (-49.5, 0), 5 * 365days)","title":"Alkalinity release"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Simulation","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"backend_size = 2920 loads the whole repeat year of JRA55 (3-hourly) into GPU memory.\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid;\n                                     biogeochemistry,\n                                     atmosphere_tracers = (; pCO₂),\n                                     forcing = (; Alk2 = alkalinity_release),\n                                     jra55_dataset = RepeatYearJRA55(),\n                                     start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1),\n                                     backend_size = 2920,\n                                     stop_time = 10 * 365days)\n\nStart from the spun-up physics on (or near) 1 January; the biogeochemical tracers keep their UnifiedBGC initial conditions.\n\npickup_restart!(simulation, DateTime(1990, 1, 1); start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1), repeat_year = true)","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Output-and-running","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"omip_diagnostics!(simulation; async = true, filename_prefix = \"amazon_alk_release\")\noaemip_diagnostics!(simulation; async = true, filename_prefix = \"amazon_alk_release\")\n\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    flush_output!(simulation) # write anything still queued\nend","title":"Output and running"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_alkalinity_release/#Results","page":"Alkalinity release","text":"(Image: Surface alkalinity and carbon uptake, background and perturbation)\n\n(Image: Alkalinity release, additional carbon uptake, and efficiency)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"biogeochemistry/#Biogeochemistry","page":"Biogeochemistry","text":"forced_ocean_simulation tries to automatically set up the additional features needed for biogeochemistry: rivers, dust, and gas exchange.\n\nIf you using OceanBioME, a NumericalEarth extension is loaded that automatically sets up CO_2 and O_2 gas exchange.\nRiver loads are added from RivR2O, co-located with the land model river mouths.\nDust is supplied by UnifiedBGC from ROMS-tools.\nBGC tracers are also initialised from UnifiedBGC.\n\nFor example, to set up an equivalent of the MITgcm DIC package, which tracks PO_4, Fe, DOP, DIC, Alk and O_2, with Mauna Loa atmospheric pCO_2:\n\nusing OceanBioME\n\narch = GPU()\ngrid = ORCA1(arch)\n\nsurface_PAR = PARFromShortwave(grid)\nlight_attenuation = PrescribedAttenuationPAR(grid, surface_PAR;\n                                             attenuation = PrescribedChlorophyllAttenuation(first_color_fraction = 0.0),\n                                             attenuation_discrete_form = true)\n\nbiogeochemistry = MITgcmDIC(grid;\n                            light_attenuation,\n                            open_bottom = false,\n                            implicit_sinking = true,\n                            store_flux = true)\n\npCO₂ = MaunaLoaCO₂(arch; dates = DateTime(1990, 1, 15):Month(1):DateTime(1990, 12, 15),\n                         start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1),\n                         period = 365days)\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid;\n                                     biogeochemistry,\n                                     atmosphere_tracers = (; pCO₂),\n                                     jra55_dataset = RepeatYearJRA55(),\n                                     start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1))\n\nMaunaLoaCO₂(arch) with no other arguments gives the monthly record for 1958–2017, cycled with the multi-year JRA55 forcing (see Forcing data); here we instead repeat 1990 to go with repeat-year forcing.\n\nWe have to use PARFromShortwave to get the downwelling radiation from the atmosphere component of the model and, since this model doesn't prognose phytoplankton, we use the chlorophyll climatology from the radiation model to attenuate the light through PrescribedAttenuationPAR with PrescribedChlorophyllAttenuation.","title":"Biogeochemistry"},{"category":"section","location":"biogeochemistry/#Alkalinity-release","page":"Biogeochemistry","text":"You can prescribe a Gaussian alkalinity release following the OAEMIP protocol by adding a GaussianInjection forcing:\n\nalkalinity_release = GaussianInjection(grid, (-49.5, 0), 0.0; duration = 20days) # grid, center (λ, φ in degrees), start time (s)\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid;\n                                     biogeochemistry,\n                                     atmosphere_tracers = (; pCO₂),\n                                     jra55_dataset = RepeatYearJRA55(),\n                                     start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1),\n                                     forcing = (; Alk2 = alkalinity_release))\n\nThe default is 1 Tmol (total_injection = 1000e12 mmol) over 1 year, in a patch with a 250 km standard deviation cut off at 3 standard deviations, in the surface cells only. See Alkalinity release at the mouth of the Amazon for a complete example.","title":"Alkalinity release"},{"category":"section","location":"configurations/#Spun-up-configurations","page":"Spun-up configurations","text":"","title":"Spun-up configurations"},{"category":"section","location":"configurations/#NumericalOcean1-JRA55_v0.2.0","page":"Spun-up configurations","text":"As built by forced_ocean_simulation(ORCA1(GPU())) with default arguments. It differs from NumericalOcean1-JRA55_v0.1.0 (the defaults at the v0.1.0 tag) in, among other things, the Redi diffusivity (1200 m² s⁻¹, was 800), the chlorophyll (the 1998–2010 SeaWiFS monthly climatology, was the year 2000) and the narrowed straits retuned towards observed transports.\n\n \nGrid eORCA1 tripolar (1degree), 70 z-star levels to 5500 m (1.5 m at the surface)\nTime step 90 minutes, split Runge–Kutta 3 (Silvestri et al., 2026), split-explicit free surface\nEquation of state TEOS-10 (Conservative Temperature and Absolute Salinity)\nMomentum advection WENO vector invariant, 5th order (Silvestri et al., 2024)\nTracer advection WENO, 7th order\nVertical mixing CATKE (Wagner et al., 2025)\nBackground diffusivity κ = max(2×10⁻⁶, 10⁻⁵ |sin φ|) m² s⁻¹ (Henyey et al., 1986), ν = 3×10⁻⁵ m² s⁻¹\nMesoscale eddies GM (Gent & McWilliams, 1990) and Redi (Redi, 1982) on triads (Griffies et al., 1998), κGM = 800 m² s⁻¹, κRedi = 1200 m² s⁻¹\nHorizontal viscosity biharmonic, ν₄ = (cell area)² / 50 days\nSea ice ClimaSeaIce, 4 thickness categories, EVP dynamics (Kimmritz et al., 2017), landfast basal stress (Lemieux et al., 2015), incremental remapping (Lipscomb & Hunke, 2004)\nAtmosphere and runoff JRA55-do 1958–2018 (Tsujino et al., 2018)\nAir-sea fluxes COARE similarity theory (Edson et al., 2013)\nIce-ocean heat flux three-equation (Holland & Jenkins, 1999)\nShortwave penetration two-band, SeaWiFS chlorophyll monthly climatology (1998–2010)\nSalinity restoring to monthly WOA, piston velocity 0.254 m day⁻¹\nInitial conditions WOA annual temperature and salinity, ECCO4 sea ice (January 1993), ocean at rest\nSpin-up 5 cycles of 1958–2018 JRA55","title":"NumericalOcean1-JRA55_v0.2.0"},{"category":"section","location":"forcing_data/#Forcing-data","page":"Forcing data","text":"To change the automatically configured data and output directories, change the value of NumericalOcean.forcing_dir, NumericalOcean.restoring_dir, NumericalOcean.staging_dir, NumericalOcean.bgc_dir, and NumericalOcean.output_dir. They are Refs, so you set them like\n\nNumericalOcean.bgc_dir[] = \"~/bgc_data/\"\n\nstaging_dir is only needed when you have a slow forcing_dir, so that data can be asynchronously loaded to a faster drive before it is needed.","title":"Forcing data"},{"category":"section","location":"forcing_data/#Normally...","page":"Forcing data","text":"When you first load this package it will create the directories data/forcing_data, data/climatology, data/output, and data/staging. When you run the model for the first time it will try to download the whole JRA55 surface dataset, which is around 700 GiB, so it is probably best to arrange for this before running, with scripts/download_forcing.jl.\n\nRepeat-year (1990) forcing is much smaller; to use it pass jra55_dataset = RepeatYearJRA55(). It is also small enough to be fully loaded into GPU memory on an H100 if you pass backend_size = 2920 (one year of 3-hourly records), which gives a meaningful speed-up.\n\nTo use the biogeochemistry setup you will also need to download UnifiedBGC and the RivR2O dataset.","title":"Normally..."},{"category":"section","location":"forcing_data/#On-Bouchet","page":"Forcing data","text":"If you are running on Bouchet the package will point to a predownloaded copy of all the data; if you cannot access it please email Jago. On Bouchet staging_dir is automatically configured to use the fast NVMe storage on the nodes, and is used if you pass staging = true to forced_ocean_simulation (although this has given minimal performance gain).","title":"On Bouchet"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Spinning-up-the-1\\degree-configuration","page":"Spin-up","text":"This example builds the NumericalOcean1-JRA55_v0.2.0 spin-up (see Spun-up configurations): the default ocean–sea ice configuration on eORCA1, run from rest through 5 cycles of 1958–2018 JRA55 forcing (5 × 21915 days, about 300 years). At around 140 simulated years per day on an H100 this takes a little over two days, so it is not run when the documentation is built.","title":"Spinning up the 1degree configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Packages","page":"Spin-up","text":"using NumericalOcean\nusing NumericalEarth\nusing Oceananigans\nusing Oceananigans.Units\nusing Dates\nusing CUDA\n\nauto_config_directories!()","title":"Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Grid","page":"Spin-up","text":"The nominal 1degree eORCA1 tripolar grid on the GPU.\n\ngrid = ORCA1(GPU())","title":"Grid"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Simulation","page":"Spin-up","text":"Everything is left at its default: multi-year JRA55 forcing from 1 January 1958, temperature and salinity initialised from the WOA annual climatology (converted to TEOS-10), sea ice from ECCO4 (January 1993), the ocean at rest, and stop_time = 5 * 21915days. There is no pickup_restart!: this run is what the restarts come from.\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid)","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Output-and-checkpoints","page":"Spin-up","text":"The OMIP diagnostics, and a checkpoint every 360 days. The \"orca\" prefix gives the orca_checkpoint_iteration*.jld2 files that pickup_restart! looks for: to restart from your own spin-up set NumericalOcean.OMIP_RESTART_DIR[] to the output directory.\n\nomip_diagnostics!(simulation; async = true)\ncheckpointer!(simulation; filename_prefix = \"orca\")","title":"Output and checkpoints"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Running","page":"Spin-up","text":"A spin-up this long will usually span several jobs: after the first, resume from the latest checkpoint with run!(simulation; pickup = true).\n\ntry\n    run!(simulation)\nfinally\n    flush_output!(simulation) # write anything still queued\nend","title":"Running"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/1degree_spinup/#Results","page":"Spin-up","text":"(Image: Surface fields in the final 10 years)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"#NumericalOcean","page":"Home","text":"Global ocean–sea ice configurations in Oceananigans, with optional biogeochemistry from OceanBioME. This configuration package assumes familiarity with Oceananigans and, to some extent, NumericalEarth.","title":"NumericalOcean"},{"category":"section","location":"#Setup","page":"Home","text":"This package depends on unreleased branches of Oceananigans, ClimaSeaIce, OceanBioME, SeawaterPolynomials, and NumericalEarth. To install, create a Julia project:\n\n~$ mkdir AnOceanProject\n~$ cd AnOceanProject\n~$ touch Project.toml\n~$ julia --project -t auto\n\nand add the branches along with NumericalOcean:\n\njulia> using Pkg\n\njulia> Pkg.add([PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/NumericalEarth/NumericalEarth.jl.git\", rev=\"jsw/omip-bgc\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/NumericalEarth/NumericalEarth.jl.git\", rev=\"jsw/omip-bgc\", subdir=\"experiments/OMIPSimulations\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/Clima/Oceananigans.jl.git\", rev=\"jsw/for-oaemip\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/jagoosw/ClimaSeaIce.jl.git\", rev=\"jsw/for-oaemip\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/OceanBioME/OceanBioME.jl.git\", rev=\"jsw/for-oaemip\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/clima/SeawaterPolynomials.jl.git\", rev=\"ss/conversion-functions\"),\n                PackageSpec(url=\"https://github.com/jagoosw/NumericalOcean\", rev=\"v0.1.0\")])\n\nThe branches have to be added first, in the same call or before NumericalOcean, because Pkg ignores the [sources] in NumericalOcean's Project.toml when it is installed as a package, and OMIPSimulations and some dependencies are not registered. This puts Oceananigans, OceanBioME and NumericalEarth in your project, so they can be used in scripts; you will also need to add CUDA to run on the GPU:\n\njulia> Pkg.add(\"CUDA\")\n\nAlternatively, clone the repository and use it as the project (julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'), where its [sources] are used directly.","title":"Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Physics: the default ocean–sea ice setup and how to modify it.\nBiogeochemistry: rivers, dust, gas exchange and alkalinity releases.\nForcing data: where the data lives and how to get it.\nRestart files: starting from the OMIP spin-up.\nSpun-up configurations: what the spun-up states were built with.\nSpinning up the 1degree configuration: building the spin-up the restarts come from.\nAlkalinity release at the mouth of the Amazon: a worked example.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Performance","page":"Home","text":"Currently we get around 90 simulated years per day with the MITgcm BGC, and 140 simulated years per day with physics only, on an H100 on Bouchet.\n\nWe have been requesting 8 CPUs with 16 GB of memory each (probably excessive) and running Julia with --threads=4 --gcthreads=4,1, which helped performance a bit.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"restarts/#Restart-files","page":"Restart files","text":"pickup_restart!(simulation, date) starts a simulation from the checkpoint of the OMIP spin-up (orca_checkpoint_iteration*.jld2 in NumericalOcean.OMIP_RESTART_DIR[], which points at the spin-up on Bouchet) whose forcing date is closest to date, and sets the model clock so the forcing is picked up where the checkpointed run had it.\n\nThe spin-up was forced with 1958–2018 JRA55 and checkpointed every 360 days, so checkpoints fall on different dates in each cycle; by default only the last cycle is searched (pass cycle = n, 0-based, for another).\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid)\n\n# the last-cycle state nearest 1 January 1958, with the clock at its time since 1958-01-01\npickup_restart!(simulation, DateTime(1958, 1, 1))\n\nFor a run with a different start_date, pass the same start_date to pickup_restart!: only checkpoints dated on or after it can be used.\n\nFor repeat-year runs pass repeat_year = true to pick the checkpoint nearest date (in any year) and set the clock to its time of year since start_date:\n\nsimulation = forced_ocean_simulation(grid; jra55_dataset = RepeatYearJRA55(), start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1))\n\npickup_restart!(simulation, DateTime(1990, 1, 1); start_date = DateTime(1990, 1, 1), repeat_year = true)\n\nOnly the model state is restored: the iteration is reset to zero, and output and callback schedules start afresh from the new time. State not in the checkpoint (e.g. biogeochemical tracers when restarting from the physics-only spin-up) keeps the values it was initialised with.","title":"Restart files"}]
}
